WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.109
Film Courage: Tropes a new screenwriter should
avoid that a more seasoned screenwriter can

00:00:05.109 --> 00:00:06.109
navigate?

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:08.730
Gordy Hoffman, Founder/Judge of the BlueCat
Screenplay Competition: Well that’s an interesting

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:09.730
question.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.960
These are like very bizarre questions?

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:20.289
Okay…I don’t think there are tropes that
a seasoned screenwriter are able to navigate

00:00:20.289 --> 00:00:22.439
that sounds like…that sounds like a red
flag.

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:23.669
“I don’t want to do that Mommy!

00:00:23.669 --> 00:00:32.050
I don’t want to do tropes I’m not supposed
to do when I’m a baby writer!”

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:34.640
It’s like we need to tell honest stuff.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.780
It needs to be original, it needs to be plausible,
it needs to be emotionally available.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.590
It always needs to be like that.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:43.640
That needs to happen on page 10.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.519
It needs to happen in the first 10 pages.

00:00:45.519 --> 00:00:48.250
It needs to happen between page 62 and 72.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:54.960
I mean it’s all over the script and we can’t
ever not traffic in that currency.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:01.539
We don’t ever want to be like “Well later
you can screw around and trick people.”

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:07.590
And obviously there are things that happen
where you have better control but I just can’t…that

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:15.070
intuitively just doesn’t feel like a seasoned
writer is ever going to be able to cheat an

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:23.729
audience in a more effective way and they
are going to learn tricks that…I mean there

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:32.280
are things with timelines and cheating time
and tricking the audience in terms of (like

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.630
letting the audience, fooling around with
the calendar and being like well all this

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:41.930
is happening and it feels like it’s happening
over a course of eight years but it feels

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.990
like it also could be happening over a year
and I there are things that are sophisticated

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.689
that happen but that’s not a trope though.

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:53.180
But that’s just structure.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:59.750
When I started off writing 25 years ago (writing
a play) having three people having a conversation

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:01.350
was confusing to me.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.640
It was like “Okay, who is talking to whom?”

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:10.489
Four people talking was difficult to hear
that conversation in my mind let alone having

00:02:10.489 --> 00:02:17.730
a 150-page script that I just wrote on contract
to with four different arcs happening in three

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.840
different locations all kind of coming together.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:27.970
It was so sophisticated that I never, it was
like way out there for something I never would

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:33.420
have been able to do that even probably 15,
10 years ago.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:34.890
It was too difficult.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:38.380
I’d still look at it and go “How did you
do this?”

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:44.790
It’s so sophisticated in terms of its structure,
keeping all the balls in the air, keeping

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:52.069
it believable and clear and not forgetting
anybody and not crowding your cast and all

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:53.430
those things.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.780
I don’t know about that question?

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.400
It’s a tricky question.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.960
I hope somebody else can figure that out because
I don’t think there are tropes that, there

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.070
probably are things that, there probably are
things that some other teacher will be able

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:17.120
to answer but I don’t want to ever promise
a new writer “Oh, you’ll be able to use

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.480
tropes.”

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.030
Which suggests something that is overused.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:27.610
“You’ll be able to use those later when
you get more seasoned.”

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.659
I was like “I don’t ever want you to do
that.

00:03:29.659 --> 00:03:35.310
I want you to always listen to your voice,
write what you believe in, write what you

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:39.150
really want to write and that’s what we
want to give our audiences and that’s what

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:43.870
we want to think about is our audiences not
like what we’re going to be able to get

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:49.370
away with later when we no everything…no…no…dangerous!

